  {
   "kind": "interpretation",
   "title": "Explanation and interpretation",
   "sort_order": 9,
   "payload": {
    "sections": [
     {
      "heading": "Question",
      "paragraphs": [
       "Is severe B. rossi disease anaemia-dominant in dogs under 12 months and inflammatory in older dogs? The package hypothesis is that age changes the form of disease more than the risk of death (the risk part is SV-05-01)."
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "What was done",
      "paragraphs": [
       "Inside each source, young dogs (under 12 months) were compared with adults on haematocrit (DS05, 55 vs 92; DS03 natural, 41 vs 60), band neutrophils (DS05; S-CB050, 50 vs 90), the SV-04 cytokine perturbation count (S-CB050 cohort 1, 33 vs 63), the share of dogs with severe anaemia and with a left shift (DS05) and days ill before presentation (DS05). Hodges-Lehmann differences with 2,000 bootstrap resamples of dogs, permutation p values (10,000), Benjamini-Hochberg over the 8 tests. A decision rule was written before the analysis. SV-07 found that the DS03 natural dogs are S-CB050 dogs, so DS03 is counted as the same source (SRC-A), never pooled and never called a replication; the independent sources are SRC-A and SRC-F (DS05). No outcome label is used in this experiment. S-CB050 and DS05 were seen before this plan, so the result is exploratory."
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "Result",
      "paragraphs": [
       "Verdict by the rule: Not supported. Haematocrit in young minus adult dogs: DS05 -0.02 L/L (-0.04 to +0.01, medians 0.17 vs 0.18), DS03 -0.06 (-0.10 to -0.03, medians 0.14 vs 0.20, q 0.007). The lower haematocrit of young dogs is clear only in the Onderstepoort dogs, and the two healthy young controls there are also below every adult control (check C1), so part of it is the normal age difference. Severe anaemia: 42% of young vs 32% of adult DS05 dogs (difference +0.10, -0.05 to +0.26).",
       "No adult excess of inflammation: bands DS05 -0.10 (-0.20 to +0.01), S-CB050 +0.00 (-0.11 to +0.13); perturbation count Cliff's delta -0.14 (-0.37 to +0.09); left shift 27% vs 41% (-0.14, -0.28 to +0.02). DS05 points the hypothesised way (fewer bands in young dogs) but every interval includes 0.",
       "Young dogs presented earlier: median 3 vs 4 days ill, HL -1 (-1 to +0), Cliff's delta -0.23 (-0.41 to -0.03), p 0.023, q 0.090. 1 of 8 tests pass q < 0.05 (DS03 haematocrit only). The 7 neutered DS05 dogs are all adults and are described, not tested."
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "What this does not show",
      "paragraphs": [
       "It does not show that age does not change the form of disease: each contrast could detect only a difference of about 0.5 SD or more. There is no outcome label in DS05 or DS03, so 'form of severe disease' is read from the whole infected population, not from the severe dogs. Age stands in for prior exposure, breed and size, which are not recorded. A between-source difference (DS03 against DS05) cannot be attributed to age: source, region, year and case mix differ."
      ]
     }
    ]
   }
